Would you want me to instead keep this issue as the broken transparency issue and instead create a new one for the resizability?

There is also another possible solution: adding the `WS_THICKFRAME` window style to the transparent window via a `Node.js addon`. Electron can obtain the native window handle, and this approach can be used as a temporary workaround until this issue is fully identified and clarified.

```js
// main.js
const { app, BrowserWindow } = require('electron');

app.whenReady().then(() => {
  const win = new BrowserWindow({
    transparent: true,
    frame: false,
  });

  // On Windows, this returns an HWND buffer
  const hwnd = win.getNativeWindowHandle();

  // Pass the HWND to native addon
  const addon = require('./build/Release/window_style_addon.node');
  addon.addThickFrame(hwnd);
});
```
```c++
#include <napi.h>
#include <windows.h>

/**
 * This code was generated by an AI (Gemini).
 * Function: AddThickFrame
 * Purpose: Dynamically adds the WS_THICKFRAME style to an Electron window to 
 * enable native resizing for frameless windows on Windows.
 */

void AddThickFrame(const Napi::CallbackInfo& info) {
    Napi::Env env = info.Env();

    // 1. Retrieve the HWND (Window Handle) from the Node.js Buffer.
    // getNativeWindowHandle() returns a buffer containing the memory address of the HWND.
    HWND hwnd = *reinterpret_cast<HWND*>(info[0].As<Napi::Buffer<char>>().Data());

    // 2. Get current window styles using GetWindowLong.
    LONG currentStyle = GetWindowLong(hwnd, GWL_STYLE);

    // 3. Apply the WS_THICKFRAME flag using a bitwise OR (|) operation.
    // This adds the "resizable" border style while preserving all existing styles.
    SetWindowLong(hwnd, GWL_STYLE, currentStyle | WS_THICKFRAME);

    // 4. Force the window to recalculate its non-client area (frame).
    // Without SWP_FRAMECHANGED, the visual change won't take effect immediately.
    // SWP_NOMOVE | SWP_NOSIZE | SWP_NOZORDER ensures we only refresh without moving the window.
    SetWindowPos(
        hwnd, 
        NULL, 
        0, 0, 0, 0, 
        SWP_NOMOVE | SWP_NOSIZE | SWP_NOZORDER | SWP_FRAMECHANGED
    );
}

Napi::Object Init(Napi::Env env, Napi::Object exports) {
    exports.Set("addThickFrame", Napi::Function::New(env, AddThickFrame));
    return exports;
}

NODE_API_MODULE(window_style_addon, Init)
```

Hello @participant1 

Thanks for commenting and offering a potential workaround.

I have learned a few more things that upset the applecart, for better or worse, but I think for the better.

I went down the path of trying to see exactly what the mechanism for resizing was and as it turns out there are two different resize modes.  When the window is frame-less, a chromium/electron specific hit-test method is used instead of the operating systems native resize handling.  There are two particular functions that gate this functionality based on `IsResisable()` and `CanResize()`, which is why this functionality broke for win32..

See: https://github.com/electron/electron/blob/5901d8a6d8bf019bf8b535cb8ffe18860bdf3ad3/shell/browser/native_window.cc#L668-L691

and:
https://github.com/electron/electron/blob/3985daa81c4da5b29c8a7fb8cc52144afc7f999d/shell/browser/ui/views/frameless_view.cc#L37-L57


If I make no other changes but to hack those two functions to believe resizing is allowed, then it makes no difference what windows style flags are set; resize just works regardless.

