And finally this bare bones electron app that changes the WS_THICKFRAME flag every 4 seconds...
```javascript
electron.app.on('ready', () => {
    const win = new electron.BrowserWindow({
        frame: false,
        transparent: true,
    });
    win.loadFile('resize-test.html');
    win.show();

    let on = true;
    setInterval(() => {
        console.warn("OKAY, flip the bit", on ? 'ON' : 'OFF');
        win.setFocusable(on);
        on = !on;
    }, 4000);
});
```

You can see in the video below that both, resizing still works and the `WS_THICKFRAME` flag, while affecting the shadows of the window, has no bearing on its' resizability.

https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/c1012781-d29d-4b7c-8bb9-7fd2efcb0dbd

Where the `WS_THICKFRAME` option does matter, is when `frame:true`.  When the frame is present the hit-test method is not used and the operating system is expected to handle this.  Without `WS_THICKFRAME` resize truly is disabled on win32.

My gut says the correct place to focus is on harmonizing `IsResizable()` and `CanResize()`, possibly with a unified implementation.  And making it clear that setting `thickFrame:false` only applies when `frame:true` as well.

I'll sleep on this and await further comment before offering a PR, but I feel a bit more confident that this can be resolved with a fairly safe patch.
